# Document review pilot

From the generated repository, run:

```sh
uv run {{python_package_command_line_name}} document_review summarize-document document-review-example/cohort.md \
  --mode compare --fixture document-review-example/fixture.json
```

Both modes receive the same source and use the same drafting prompt.
The baseline drafts and self-revises. Reviewed mode independently checks
the draft and, when needed, revises once and reviews again. Each response
must match the requested JSON schema and cite valid source line ranges.
A second review that still finds defects produces `unresolved` with the
findings. Treat line validation as a structural check. Read the cited text
to verify that it supports the claim.

The fixture deliberately leaves an error in the baseline and fixes it in
reviewed mode.

| Mode | Keeps “opt-in cohort only” | Incorrectly says “all users” | Calls |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Baseline | No | Yes | 2 |
| Reviewed | Yes | No | 4 |

This scripted comparison checks the execution and scoring paths. To measure
model quality, run the comparison on held-out documents with the same model
for both modes. Score the final outputs against a human-written list of
required facts, qualifiers, and known errors. Record correct claims lost
during revision, reviewer false positives, calls, and elapsed time. The
adapter can record provider token usage separately.

## Model adapter

Replace `--fixture` with `--model-command ./path-to-executable`. The CLI
starts the executable once per step, with a 30-second timeout. It writes one
JSON object to stdin with `operation`, `prompt`, and `schema`. The adapter
should return only the JSON response on stdout and exit successfully. Use
the same model settings for both modes. The adapter receives the document
text, so choose an adapter appropriate for the document's sensitivity.
